WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.000
- By some measures, our
economy is doing great.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.660
- Food, fuel, everything costs too much.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.210
- [Narrator] Both of these
candidates are kind of right.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.540
Leading into the midterm
elections inflation is high,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.760
the highest in the last 40 years

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.800
and gas prices are volatile,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.210
though they've come down recently,

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.830
there's still a lot of uncertainty

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.810
about where they're going next.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.750
But then again, there's
incredibly low unemployment,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.360
just look at the number
of job openings right now.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.180
Things are expensive,
but people are working.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.550
Yet most economists polled
by the Wall Street Journal

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.860
believe the US is headed
towards a recession

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:35.817
in the next year.

00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:38.670
- The economy is always a
big deal in any election.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.680
I mean, as James Carville once famously

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.060
coined the term in 1992.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.350
- It's the economy stupid.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.450
- This year is obviously a year

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.340
where we're seeing a lot of interest,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.820
a lot of intensity and
debate about the economy.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.070
- [Narrator] Here's how the
economy actually is doing

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.160
and why it matters for
the November midterms.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.710
- I can't tell you how many
times I've seen gas pumps

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.770
and grocery store aisles
in political ads this year.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.080
- [Narrator] Inflation is one
of the big economic measures

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.640
you can feel personally.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.060
- And I feel the pain too because
I do the grocery shopping.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.740
- [Narrator] And not just
at the grocery store,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.780
although the price of food has gone up

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.580
more than most things.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.560
If you look at median weekly earnings

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.570
people are making less than they were

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.900
during the last election,
if adjusted for inflation.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.870
- Inflation is sort of
taking a bite out of,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.260
you know, what otherwise
would be a fairly strong

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.050
and robust economy coming
out of the pandemic.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.390
- [Narrator] Republicans
are largely blaming.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.560
- [Reporter 1] Man's reckless spending.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.820
- [Reporter 2] Trillions in spending.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.320
- Out of control spending.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.730
- [Narrator] While Democrats point out.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:39.720
- The supply chain.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.760
- More microchips here in America

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.500
to get the supply chain moving again.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.810
- We'll beat inflation one chip at a time.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.400
- There's a lot of reasons for why

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.220
inflation is at this super high level.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.100
You know, it has to do
with global supply chains,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.260
it has to do with the pandemic,

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.050
it has to do with energy prices.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.870
You know, the situation in Ukraine,

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:05.460
all of that has contributed to
astronomical price increases

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.860
and so both parties are trying to point

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.820
to certain aspects of that bigger formula.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.960
- [Narrator] Another place voters

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.610
have felt the economic pressure.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.430
- If you're fed up every time you fill up.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.660
- [Narrator] Let's take a closer look

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.100
at that gas price chart.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.250
Here is where the Russian
invasion of Ukraine began.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.370
Experts largely blamed that
war for the initial increase

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.950
but it stayed high for
a number of reasons.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.060
Republicans blame.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.100
- [Reporter 3] Our energy
production has been crippled.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.320
- They've cratered our
domestic energy production.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.670
- [Narrator] While Democrats blame.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.440
- [Reporter 4] Oil companies
gouging us at the pump.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.330
- [Reporter 5] Price gouging by big oil.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.550
- Both sides have some points

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.890
but there's no sort of easy answer.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.060
- [Narrator] Analysts pointed things

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.070
beyond the Biden administration's control,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.230
like maintenance at refineries.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.910
- Some of these things
just have to do with,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.560
you know, global supply chains.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.820
Coming out of the pandemic,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.140
we're just obviously in a really
weird time for the economy.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.690
- [Narrator] Americans'
frustration over rising prices

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.150
show up in arguably the
most important factor

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.420
going into the election, how people feel.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.940
This is the Consumer Sentiment Index,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.520
it measures how optimistic or pessimistic

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.920
people are feeling about the economy.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.650
In the last 40 years, every
time the President's party

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.500
has lost a large number
of seats in the midterms,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.690
it was after a period where Americans felt

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.790
pessimistic about the economy.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.960
- You never want voters
to be in a grumpy mood

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.690
when you're the party in power.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.550
It's easier for them to sort of cast

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.710
that ballot in opposition.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.540
- [Narrator] And right
now people feel worse

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.460
about the economy than they even did

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.470
during the Great Recession.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.390
Even though there are far fewer people

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.400
filing unemployment claims today

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.650
than there were during that recession.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.900
There are also more jobs being added today

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.543
than even during the Great
Recession's recovery.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.440
By these measures, the economy is good,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.960
but where people feel
the economy the most,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.720
the grocery store and the
gas station, it's not.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.520
- For voters, they're definitely hearing,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.380
you know, the blame game from both sides

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.670
and ultimately for them,
it's gonna come down,

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.194
which party do you think has
the best chance of fixing it?

00:04:10.194 --> 00:04:12.777
(upbeat music)

